<svelte:head><title>Privacy and participation · Voynich@home</title><meta name="description" content="See what Voynich@home stores when you volunteer as a guest or with an account, publish a profile, or delete your account."/></svelte:head>
<div class="page narrow prose"><p class="eyebrow">Last updated · 9 September 2026</p><h1>Privacy and participation</h1><p class="lede">The site starts computation only after you click. You can volunteer without an account, and public profiles are opt-in.</p><h2>Guest participation</h2><p>An HTTP-only, same-site cookie identifies your guest session for up to 90 days. The site marks it secure on HTTPS, and the server stores a hash of its proof instead of the cookie value. Your browser stores checkpoints and unsent results in IndexedDB. Clearing browser data prevents you from resuming that work.</p><h2>Accounts and public profiles</h2><p>If you sign in, the authentication service stores your provider account, name, email, and sessions. It encrypts provider tokens when a provider returns them. Session records may include your IP address and browser information. Contribution rankings do not publish these details.</p><p>You must choose public visibility before the site publishes your display name, checked credit, or team membership. Team names are public. The site attaches guest contributions to an account only when the current browser proves control of that guest session.</p><h2>Deletion and research records</h2><p>Deleting your account removes the account, sign-in sessions, and public profile. The site detaches contribution records, records the deletion, and revokes attached guest-session proofs. Research records remain without your account identity so others can reproduce published experiments.</p><h2>Operating data</h2><p>The hosting provider processes network requests and operating logs. To enforce short-lived abuse limits, the application converts each IP address into a new daily digest and also counts requests by guest session. The operator can inspect work failures and moderation records. The project uses no advertising trackers and does not sell contribution profiles.</p><p>The project keeps daily backups for 30 days, so a backup may contain an earlier account record during that period. Before restoring public access from an older backup, the operator must reapply the deletion records.</p><h2>Software trust</h2><p>Approved worker modules use published digests and restricted imports. These checks detect mismatched code. A compromised website could still replace its own checking code, so a verified native release provides a separate reproduction path.</p><p><a href="/account">Manage your sessions, profile, or account →</a></p><p class="small muted">For a privacy request, open an issue in the project’s <a href="https://github.com/javidmardanov/VoynichAtHome/issues">GitHub issue tracker ↗</a>.</p></div>
